From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:54:12 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] client: the loading screen can wait for the terrain
 around the player

In singleplayer the loading screen ended once the textures and nodes were ready, and only then did
the server place the player and send the map: the world showed building itself around them, which
looks unfinished. With loading_terrain_radius (nodes, 0 = as before) the screen stays, as "Loading
terrain...", until the server has sent, culled or left out as air every block within that radius
(its RemoteClient's nearest unsent distance, read through the local server; or a full send, when
the radius reaches past what it sends) and the client has made every mesh asked for, the simplified
far ones too, for half a second; or loading_terrain_timeout seconds pass. The player's pitch and yaw
now come with TOCLIENT_MOVE_PLAYER into the local player, so the position sent meanwhile asks for
the blocks the player will see first; the camera's wanted range is set before the first frame, and
once the player is placed the camera and the draw list are updated as each frame does, which is
what asks for the far meshes past client_lod_distance. The time taken goes to the log ("Terrain
within N nodes loaded after T s").

The steps before it then fill the first 60 % of the bar (RenderingEngine::setLoadScreenRange maps a
load screen's 0-100 % into a part of the bar) and the terrain the rest; the server starts its sweep
again when the player moves to another block or turns, so the bar shows the farthest it got and
never goes back.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

